      "content": "Condition I: Exactly Two Vertices of Odd Degrees"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "As a reminder, a vertex has an odd degree if it has an odd number of edges. For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Graph With Two Edges of Odd Degree",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, if there are exactly two nodes of odd degrees, we can simply put them as the endpoints of the route. Here,"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "The two odd-degree vertices serve as the start and end points of the trail.",
        "As you traverse the graph, every time you enter a vertex (other than the starting and ending vertices), you can always leave it along another edge since they have even degrees.",
        "The trail will start at one odd-degree vertex and end at the other, ensuring every edge is traversed exactly once."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Such routes that visit every edge in a graph only once are called the Euler trail or Euler path."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Next, we'll look at the second condition for an Euler trail."
    },
